CUT(1)                         P32W Unix                          CUT(1)

NAME
       cut - write selected parts of lines

SYNOPSIS
       cut -b list [-n] [file ...]
       cut -c list [file ...]
       cut -f list [-d delim] [-s] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       cut writes the selected bytes, characters or fields of each
       line of the files (the standard input if none is named, or
       for a file named -). Lines may be of any length; a last line
       without a newline is given one.

       A list is numbers and ranges separated by commas or blanks
       (a list with blanks must be quoted):

       N      the Nth (counting from 1)
       N-M    the Nth to the Mth
       N-     the Nth to the end of the line
       -M     the first to the Mth

       The parts are written in the order they come in the line,
       each once, whatever the order of the list.

OPTIONS
       -b list
              the bytes in list
       -c list
              the characters in list (the same as bytes)
       -f list
              the fields in list; fields are separated by the
              delimiter (a tab), and the fields written are
              separated by it too. A line with no delimiter is
              written whole, unless -s is given.
       -d delim
              with -f: the delimiter is the character delim
       -s     with -f: leave out lines that have no delimiter
       -n     with -b: accepted and ignored (characters are bytes)

       Exactly one of -b, -c and -f must be given. The list or
       delimiter may follow its option directly (-f2, -d:).

EXAMPLES
       cut -d: -f1,7 /etc/passwd     names and shells
       cut -c1-8 file                the first 8 characters
       ls -l | cut -c 30-            the end of each line

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or a bad list, or if a file could not
       be opened (cut says why and goes on with the others).

SEE ALSO
       paste(1), join(1), tr(1), grep(1)

BUGS
       There is no --output-delimiter or --complement. Characters
       are bytes.
